00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,776
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,956 --> 00:00:03,678
What should your net worth be at

3
00:00:03,678 --> 00:00:04,162
your age?

4
00:00:04,342 --> 00:00:06,429
It's one of the most searched money

5
00:00:06,429 --> 00:00:07,980
questions on the internet,

6
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:10,067
and most of the answers you'll find

7
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:11,737
are either scary or useless.

8
00:00:11,917 --> 00:00:14,947
So today, real numbers from the Federal

9
00:00:14,947 --> 00:00:16,968
Reserve, decade by decade.

10
00:00:17,148 --> 00:00:18,362
And more importantly,

11
00:00:18,362 --> 00:00:19,980
what to do if you're behind.

12
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:21,333
Because if you are,

13
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:22,691
you're far from alone.

14
00:00:23,041 --> 00:00:24,284
Net worth is simple.

15
00:00:24,464 --> 00:00:25,622
Everything you own,

16
00:00:25,622 --> 00:00:27,146
minus everything you owe.

17
00:00:27,326 --> 00:00:30,312
Savings, investments, retirement accounts,

18
00:00:30,312 --> 00:00:32,019
the equity in your home,

19
00:00:32,019 --> 00:00:33,370
minus credit cards,

20
00:00:33,370 --> 00:00:36,428
car loans, student loans and your mortgage.

21
00:00:36,778 --> 00:00:39,085
Why does this one number matter so

22
00:00:39,085 --> 00:00:39,425
much?

23
00:00:39,605 --> 00:00:41,509
Because income can fool you.

24
00:00:41,689 --> 00:00:43,713
Plenty of people earn a lot and

25
00:00:43,713 --> 00:00:44,823
keep very little.

26
00:00:45,003 --> 00:00:47,221
Net worth shows what you've actually kept.

27
00:00:47,401 --> 00:00:49,928
It's the scoreboard for every money decision

28
00:00:49,928 --> 00:00:50,904
you've ever made,

29
00:00:50,904 --> 00:00:52,512
and the good news is that it

30
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:54,866
can change faster than most people think,

31
00:00:54,866 --> 00:00:57,049
once you start paying attention to it.

32
00:00:57,399 --> 00:00:59,593
We'll look at it from three angles.

33
00:00:59,773 --> 00:01:01,057
First, the numbers:

34
00:01:01,057 --> 00:01:04,031
what typical Americans actually have at each

35
00:01:04,031 --> 00:01:06,329
age, and the rules of thumb people

36
00:01:06,329 --> 00:01:06,599
use.

37
00:01:06,779 --> 00:01:08,111
Then the psychology:

38
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:10,773
why comparing yourself can help or hurt.

39
00:01:10,953 --> 00:01:12,721
And finally the playbook:

40
00:01:12,721 --> 00:01:14,418
exactly how to catch up,

41
00:01:14,418 --> 00:01:15,690
whatever your age.

42
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:17,893
Grab your own number if you know

43
00:01:17,893 --> 00:01:19,221
it, or a rough guess,

44
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:21,118
and let's see where you stand.

45
00:01:21,467 --> 00:01:23,157
And stay until the end,

46
00:01:23,157 --> 00:01:25,802
because the catch up playbook in the

47
00:01:25,802 --> 00:01:28,154
last part of this video works at

48
00:01:28,154 --> 00:01:30,946
any age, whether you're twenty five or

49
00:01:30,946 --> 00:01:31,754
sixty five.

50
00:01:31,934 --> 00:01:34,007
First, where the numbers come from,

51
00:01:34,007 --> 00:01:35,962
because there's a lot of bad data

52
00:01:35,962 --> 00:01:36,555
out there.

53
00:01:36,904 --> 00:01:39,040
The net worth figures in this video

54
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,542
come from the Federal Reserve's Survey of

55
00:01:41,542 --> 00:01:42,640
Consumer Finances.

56
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:45,301
It's the most detailed survey of American

57
00:01:45,301 --> 00:01:47,721
household wealth, run every three years,

58
00:01:47,721 --> 00:01:50,142
and the latest full results cover twenty

59
00:01:50,142 --> 00:01:50,807
twenty two.

60
00:01:50,987 --> 00:01:52,941
The numbers are grouped by the age

61
00:01:52,941 --> 00:01:54,436
of the head of the family.

62
00:01:54,616 --> 00:01:57,759
The salary multiples come from Fidelity Investments'

63
00:01:57,759 --> 00:02:00,661
retirement guidelines, and the net worth formula

64
00:02:00,661 --> 00:02:03,079
comes from the book The Millionaire Next

65
00:02:03,079 --> 00:02:03,381
Door.

66
00:02:03,731 --> 00:02:05,067
Two important notes.

67
00:02:05,247 --> 00:02:06,786
These are survey numbers,

68
00:02:06,786 --> 00:02:08,079
so they're estimates.

69
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:10,149
And they include home equity,

70
00:02:10,149 --> 00:02:12,039
which is a big part of wealth

71
00:02:12,039 --> 00:02:13,212
for many families,

72
00:02:13,212 --> 00:02:15,492
but not money you can easily spend.

73
00:02:15,672 --> 00:02:18,557
Growth examples use a seven percent average

74
00:02:18,557 --> 00:02:20,771
yearly return as an illustration,

75
00:02:20,771 --> 00:02:21,710
not a promise.

76
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:23,251
This is education,

77
00:02:23,251 --> 00:02:24,839
not financial advice.

78
00:02:25,188 --> 00:02:25,885
Angle one.

79
00:02:26,065 --> 00:02:26,820
The numbers.

80
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,925
Before we go decade by decade,

81
00:02:28,925 --> 00:02:31,429
you need to know the difference between

82
00:02:31,429 --> 00:02:33,547
two words that change everything:

83
00:02:33,547 --> 00:02:34,767
median and average.

84
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:36,432
In twenty twenty two,

85
00:02:36,432 --> 00:02:38,687
the median net worth of all American

86
00:02:38,687 --> 00:02:41,255
families was about one hundred ninety two

87
00:02:41,255 --> 00:02:43,197
thousand seven hundred dollars.

88
00:02:43,377 --> 00:02:45,679
The average was about one million sixty

89
00:02:45,679 --> 00:02:46,210
thousand.

90
00:02:46,559 --> 00:02:48,591
Median means the family right in the

91
00:02:48,591 --> 00:02:48,986
middle.

92
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:50,298
Half have more,

93
00:02:50,298 --> 00:02:51,430
half have less.

94
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:54,272
The average adds everyone up and divides,

95
00:02:54,272 --> 00:02:56,739
so a small number of extremely wealthy

96
00:02:56,739 --> 00:02:58,297
families pull it way up.

97
00:02:58,477 --> 00:03:00,559
That's why the average is more than

98
00:03:00,559 --> 00:03:01,867
five times the median.

99
00:03:02,047 --> 00:03:04,242
When you see a headline saying the

100
00:03:04,242 --> 00:03:06,695
average American has some huge number,

101
00:03:06,695 --> 00:03:08,696
it's almost always the average,

102
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:11,278
and it doesn't describe a typical family

103
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:11,730
at all.

104
00:03:11,910 --> 00:03:12,723
In this video,

105
00:03:12,723 --> 00:03:14,058
we focus on the median.

106
00:03:14,407 --> 00:03:17,063
Next, a popular rule of thumb from

107
00:03:17,063 --> 00:03:18,703
Fidelity Investments.

108
00:03:18,883 --> 00:03:21,819
They suggest aiming to have saved about

109
00:03:21,819 --> 00:03:24,228
one times your salary by thirty,

110
00:03:24,228 --> 00:03:25,809
three times by forty,

111
00:03:25,809 --> 00:03:27,240
six times by fifty,

112
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:28,821
eight times by sixty,

113
00:03:28,821 --> 00:03:31,004
and ten times by sixty seven.

114
00:03:31,184 --> 00:03:33,089
That's retirement savings,

115
00:03:33,089 --> 00:03:34,555
not total net worth,

116
00:03:34,555 --> 00:03:36,094
and it's a guideline,

117
00:03:36,094 --> 00:03:36,826
not a law.

118
00:03:37,006 --> 00:03:38,446
But it gives you a target that

119
00:03:38,446 --> 00:03:39,549
grows with your income.

120
00:03:39,898 --> 00:03:42,521
For someone earning sixty thousand dollars a

121
00:03:42,521 --> 00:03:45,323
year, Fidelity's guideline would mean about one

122
00:03:45,323 --> 00:03:48,482
hundred eighty thousand dollars in retirement savings

123
00:03:48,482 --> 00:03:50,807
by forty, and about three hundred sixty

124
00:03:50,807 --> 00:03:51,880
thousand by fifty.

125
00:03:52,229 --> 00:03:54,744
And there's an older formula from Thomas

126
00:03:54,744 --> 00:03:56,818
Stanley and William Danko's book,

127
00:03:56,818 --> 00:03:58,452
The Millionaire Next Door.

128
00:03:58,632 --> 00:04:01,486
Multiply your age by your yearly pretax

129
00:04:01,486 --> 00:04:03,462
income, then divide by ten.

130
00:04:03,642 --> 00:04:05,161
That's what they called your expected net

131
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:05,384
worth.

132
00:04:05,564 --> 00:04:07,965
A forty year old earning seventy thousand

133
00:04:07,965 --> 00:04:10,308
dollars would have an expected net worth

134
00:04:10,308 --> 00:04:12,123
of two hundred eighty thousand.

135
00:04:12,303 --> 00:04:13,410
Above double that,

136
00:04:13,410 --> 00:04:16,053
they called you a prodigious accumulator of

137
00:04:16,053 --> 00:04:16,483
wealth.

138
00:04:16,663 --> 00:04:19,113
Below half, an under accumulator.

139
00:04:19,462 --> 00:04:21,388
These rules disagree a bit,

140
00:04:21,388 --> 00:04:22,957
and they're all rough.

141
00:04:23,137 --> 00:04:23,880
That's fine.

142
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:25,973
The point isn't to hit an exact

143
00:04:25,973 --> 00:04:26,405
number.

144
00:04:26,585 --> 00:04:28,358
It's to know roughly where you are,

145
00:04:28,358 --> 00:04:30,282
and whether you're moving in the right

146
00:04:30,282 --> 00:04:30,788
direction.

147
00:04:30,968 --> 00:04:32,400
Now, decade by decade,

148
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:35,004
starting with the age group where almost

149
00:04:35,004 --> 00:04:36,436
everyone feels behind.

150
00:04:36,786 --> 00:04:39,320
Families headed by someone under thirty five

151
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:41,105
had a median net worth of about

152
00:04:41,105 --> 00:04:43,696
thirty nine thousand dollars in twenty twenty

153
00:04:43,696 --> 00:04:43,927
two.

154
00:04:44,277 --> 00:04:46,076
Thirty nine thousand dollars.

155
00:04:46,256 --> 00:04:48,027
If you're in your twenties or early

156
00:04:48,027 --> 00:04:49,898
thirties and you have less than that,

157
00:04:49,898 --> 00:04:50,505
don't panic.

158
00:04:50,685 --> 00:04:53,027
Many people in this group are just

159
00:04:53,027 --> 00:04:55,989
starting careers, paying off student loans,

160
00:04:55,989 --> 00:04:57,849
and buying their first car.

161
00:04:58,029 --> 00:05:00,303
Plenty have a negative net worth,

162
00:05:00,303 --> 00:05:02,440
especially right after college.

163
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:04,826
This decade isn't about having a lot.

164
00:05:05,006 --> 00:05:06,272
It's about the direction.

165
00:05:06,621 --> 00:05:09,125
What matters most in this decade is

166
00:05:09,125 --> 00:05:10,627
habits, not balances.

167
00:05:10,807 --> 00:05:12,514
Building an emergency fund.

168
00:05:12,694 --> 00:05:14,528
Getting the full employer match.

169
00:05:14,708 --> 00:05:16,275
Avoiding high interest debt.

170
00:05:16,455 --> 00:05:17,924
Starting to invest,

171
00:05:17,924 --> 00:05:19,393
even small amounts.

172
00:05:19,573 --> 00:05:22,348
Because money invested in your twenties has

173
00:05:22,348 --> 00:05:23,381
decades to grow.

174
00:05:23,561 --> 00:05:25,593
At a seven percent illustration,

175
00:05:25,593 --> 00:05:28,261
every dollar invested at twenty five could

176
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:31,119
become roughly fifteen dollars by sixty five.

177
00:05:31,468 --> 00:05:32,783
If you're behind here,

178
00:05:32,783 --> 00:05:34,575
the fix is simpler than at any

179
00:05:34,575 --> 00:05:35,172
other age.

180
00:05:35,352 --> 00:05:37,848
You have the most valuable asset in

181
00:05:37,848 --> 00:05:38,847
finance: time.

182
00:05:39,027 --> 00:05:41,159
Focus on raising your income,

183
00:05:41,159 --> 00:05:44,762
because early career raises compound for decades.

184
00:05:44,942 --> 00:05:45,888
Learn a skill,

185
00:05:45,888 --> 00:05:48,117
switch jobs if the pay is better,

186
00:05:48,117 --> 00:05:50,143
and keep your fixed costs low.

187
00:05:50,323 --> 00:05:52,307
A modest apartment and an older car

188
00:05:52,307 --> 00:05:54,462
in your twenties can be worth hundreds

189
00:05:54,462 --> 00:05:56,616
of thousands of dollars by retirement.

190
00:05:56,965 --> 00:05:58,394
Your move this decade:

191
00:05:58,394 --> 00:06:00,343
get your net worth above zero,

192
00:06:00,343 --> 00:06:02,616
then above one times your salary by

193
00:06:02,616 --> 00:06:03,525
around thirty.

194
00:06:03,705 --> 00:06:06,379
Invest a fixed percentage of every paycheck

195
00:06:06,379 --> 00:06:09,487
automatically, starting with at least the employer

196
00:06:09,487 --> 00:06:11,601
match, and raise it by one percent

197
00:06:11,601 --> 00:06:12,285
every year.

198
00:06:12,465 --> 00:06:13,660
If you do just that,

199
00:06:13,660 --> 00:06:16,168
you'll likely end your thirties well ahead

200
00:06:16,168 --> 00:06:17,004
of the median.

201
00:06:17,354 --> 00:06:19,796
For families headed by someone thirty five

202
00:06:19,796 --> 00:06:20,610
to forty four,

203
00:06:20,610 --> 00:06:22,587
the median net worth was about one

204
00:06:22,587 --> 00:06:24,738
hundred thirty five thousand dollars.

205
00:06:25,088 --> 00:06:26,249
Look at that jump.

206
00:06:26,429 --> 00:06:29,139
From about thirty nine thousand to about

207
00:06:29,139 --> 00:06:31,375
one hundred thirty five thousand,

208
00:06:31,375 --> 00:06:33,339
more than three times higher,

209
00:06:33,339 --> 00:06:34,626
in just one decade.

210
00:06:34,806 --> 00:06:37,693
That's what happens when people's incomes rise,

211
00:06:37,693 --> 00:06:38,860
they pay down debt,

212
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:40,272
they build home equity,

213
00:06:40,272 --> 00:06:42,852
and their early investments start to grow.

214
00:06:43,032 --> 00:06:44,977
This is often the decade where wealth

215
00:06:44,977 --> 00:06:45,923
starts to show up.

216
00:06:46,272 --> 00:06:48,893
What matters in this decade is protecting

217
00:06:48,893 --> 00:06:51,322
your savings rate while life gets more

218
00:06:51,322 --> 00:06:51,961
expensive.

219
00:06:52,141 --> 00:06:54,076
Kids, bigger homes,

220
00:06:54,076 --> 00:06:55,706
childcare, cars.

221
00:06:55,886 --> 00:06:57,735
These are also the years of peak

222
00:06:57,735 --> 00:06:58,660
lifestyle creep.

223
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:00,766
The families who come out of this

224
00:07:00,766 --> 00:07:02,983
decade strong are usually the ones who

225
00:07:02,983 --> 00:07:05,433
let their lifestyle grow slower than their

226
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:08,175
income, and kept investing through every raise.

227
00:07:08,524 --> 00:07:10,771
If you're behind in your late thirties

228
00:07:10,771 --> 00:07:11,776
or early forties,

229
00:07:11,776 --> 00:07:13,845
you still have twenty five years or

230
00:07:13,845 --> 00:07:16,268
more before a traditional retirement age.

231
00:07:16,448 --> 00:07:19,110
That's plenty of time for compounding to

232
00:07:19,110 --> 00:07:20,175
do serious work.

233
00:07:20,355 --> 00:07:22,275
The key is to increase what you

234
00:07:22,275 --> 00:07:24,070
invest, not just maintain it.

235
00:07:24,250 --> 00:07:26,476
Look at your three biggest costs,

236
00:07:26,476 --> 00:07:28,365
housing, cars and childcare,

237
00:07:28,365 --> 00:07:30,996
because that's where the real money is.

238
00:07:31,345 --> 00:07:32,751
Your move this decade:

239
00:07:32,751 --> 00:07:35,179
aim for around three times your salary

240
00:07:35,179 --> 00:07:37,160
in retirement savings by forty,

241
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:37,862
if you can,

242
00:07:37,862 --> 00:07:40,482
and push your savings rate toward fifteen

243
00:07:40,482 --> 00:07:41,504
percent or more.

244
00:07:41,684 --> 00:07:43,950
Make sure you have term life insurance

245
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:45,918
if anyone depends on your income,

246
00:07:45,918 --> 00:07:46,932
and a basic will.

247
00:07:47,112 --> 00:07:48,277
Those aren't exciting,

248
00:07:48,277 --> 00:07:50,607
but they protect everything you're building.

249
00:07:50,957 --> 00:07:53,461
For families headed by someone forty five

250
00:07:53,461 --> 00:07:54,316
to fifty four,

251
00:07:54,316 --> 00:07:56,394
the median net worth was about two

252
00:07:56,394 --> 00:07:58,654
hundred forty seven thousand dollars.

253
00:07:59,003 --> 00:08:01,871
Around two hundred forty seven thousand dollars.

254
00:08:02,051 --> 00:08:03,137
For many families,

255
00:08:03,137 --> 00:08:04,765
a big piece of this is home

256
00:08:04,765 --> 00:08:07,117
equity, since people in this group have

257
00:08:07,117 --> 00:08:09,168
often owned their homes for years.

258
00:08:09,348 --> 00:08:11,967
Their retirement accounts have also had decades

259
00:08:11,967 --> 00:08:12,413
to grow.

260
00:08:12,593 --> 00:08:14,561
This is where the gap between people

261
00:08:14,561 --> 00:08:16,693
who started early and people who didn't

262
00:08:16,693 --> 00:08:17,841
becomes very visible.

263
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:20,222
What matters most in this decade is

264
00:08:20,222 --> 00:08:22,428
that these are often your peak earning

265
00:08:22,428 --> 00:08:22,776
years.

266
00:08:22,956 --> 00:08:24,437
Kids may be getting older,

267
00:08:24,437 --> 00:08:26,146
the mortgage may be shrinking,

268
00:08:26,146 --> 00:08:28,082
and your income may be the highest

269
00:08:28,082 --> 00:08:28,994
it will ever be.

270
00:08:29,174 --> 00:08:30,360
That creates a window,

271
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:31,438
maybe fifteen years,

272
00:08:31,438 --> 00:08:33,109
where you can save more than at

273
00:08:33,109 --> 00:08:34,619
any other time in your life.

274
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:35,321
Use it.

275
00:08:35,671 --> 00:08:36,759
If you're behind,

276
00:08:36,759 --> 00:08:38,295
here's the honest truth.

277
00:08:38,475 --> 00:08:40,251
You can't get back the years you

278
00:08:40,251 --> 00:08:41,971
lost, but you can make the next

279
00:08:41,971 --> 00:08:42,748
fifteen count.

280
00:08:42,928 --> 00:08:44,582
Starting at age fifty,

281
00:08:44,582 --> 00:08:47,817
the IRS allows extra catch up contributions

282
00:08:47,817 --> 00:08:50,524
to retirement accounts on top of the

283
00:08:50,524 --> 00:08:51,577
normal limits.

284
00:08:51,757 --> 00:08:53,253
Check the current amounts,

285
00:08:53,253 --> 00:08:54,404
because they change.

286
00:08:54,584 --> 00:08:57,253
Every dollar of catch up contribution is

287
00:08:57,253 --> 00:08:59,588
a dollar that grows tax advantaged.

288
00:08:59,938 --> 00:09:01,306
Your move this decade:

289
00:09:01,306 --> 00:09:03,358
aim to put twenty percent or more

290
00:09:03,358 --> 00:09:05,970
of your income toward retirement if you're

291
00:09:05,970 --> 00:09:06,405
behind.

292
00:09:06,585 --> 00:09:08,826
Pay off any remaining high interest debt.

293
00:09:09,006 --> 00:09:11,465
Avoid taking on a new mortgage or

294
00:09:11,465 --> 00:09:13,627
big car loans now if you can.

295
00:09:13,807 --> 00:09:14,906
And get a clear estimate of your

296
00:09:14,906 --> 00:09:16,246
Social Security benefit at ssa dot gov,

297
00:09:16,246 --> 00:09:17,620
because that number changes how much you

298
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:18,033
really need.

299
00:09:18,382 --> 00:09:19,451
Quick pause.

300
00:09:19,631 --> 00:09:21,879
If you're finding your decade useful,

301
00:09:21,879 --> 00:09:22,487
subscribe.

302
00:09:22,667 --> 00:09:25,286
This channel goes step by step through

303
00:09:25,286 --> 00:09:27,424
every stage of building wealth,

304
00:09:27,424 --> 00:09:29,905
and new videos come out every couple

305
00:09:29,905 --> 00:09:30,457
of days.

306
00:09:30,637 --> 00:09:32,399
It's free, and it helps more than

307
00:09:32,399 --> 00:09:33,040
you'd think.

308
00:09:33,220 --> 00:09:35,925
Now, the decades closest to retirement.

309
00:09:36,275 --> 00:09:38,895
For families headed by someone fifty five

310
00:09:38,895 --> 00:09:39,790
to sixty four,

311
00:09:39,790 --> 00:09:42,090
the median net worth was about three

312
00:09:42,090 --> 00:09:44,391
hundred sixty four thousand dollars.

313
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:47,681
Around three hundred sixty four thousand dollars,

314
00:09:47,681 --> 00:09:50,081
right before retirement for many people.

315
00:09:50,261 --> 00:09:51,934
That may sound like a lot,

316
00:09:51,934 --> 00:09:54,185
but remember, it includes the home.

317
00:09:54,365 --> 00:09:56,562
The money available to live on is

318
00:09:56,562 --> 00:09:57,628
often much less.

319
00:09:57,808 --> 00:09:59,776
And with the four percent rule of

320
00:09:59,776 --> 00:10:02,757
thumb, two hundred thousand dollars of investments

321
00:10:02,757 --> 00:10:05,381
supports only about eight thousand dollars a

322
00:10:05,381 --> 00:10:05,679
year.

323
00:10:06,029 --> 00:10:08,166
What matters in this decade is the

324
00:10:08,166 --> 00:10:09,675
plan for the transition.

325
00:10:09,855 --> 00:10:11,840
When will you claim Social Security?

326
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:14,186
How will you pay for health insurance

327
00:10:14,186 --> 00:10:16,351
before Medicare starts at sixty five?

328
00:10:16,531 --> 00:10:17,773
Will you downsize?

329
00:10:17,953 --> 00:10:19,611
Will you work part time for a

330
00:10:19,611 --> 00:10:20,182
few years?

331
00:10:20,362 --> 00:10:22,701
These decisions can matter as much as

332
00:10:22,701 --> 00:10:24,344
anything you saved before.

333
00:10:24,694 --> 00:10:26,863
If you're behind at fifty five or

334
00:10:26,863 --> 00:10:28,770
sixty, you have real options.

335
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:31,390
Working even two or three more years

336
00:10:31,390 --> 00:10:33,220
can make a huge difference:

337
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:34,643
more years of saving,

338
00:10:34,643 --> 00:10:36,473
fewer years of withdrawals,

339
00:10:36,473 --> 00:10:38,981
and a bigger Social Security check if

340
00:10:38,981 --> 00:10:40,269
you delay claiming.

341
00:10:40,449 --> 00:10:44,410
The Social Security Administration explains that benefits

342
00:10:44,410 --> 00:10:46,702
grow for each year you delay past

343
00:10:46,702 --> 00:10:48,092
full retirement age,

344
00:10:48,092 --> 00:10:49,342
up to age seventy.

345
00:10:49,692 --> 00:10:51,134
Your move this decade:

346
00:10:51,134 --> 00:10:53,559
max out catch up contributions if you

347
00:10:53,559 --> 00:10:56,114
can, plan your health insurance bridge,

348
00:10:56,114 --> 00:10:59,456
and decide your Social Security strategy carefully.

349
00:10:59,636 --> 00:11:02,988
Many people also reduce investment risk gradually

350
00:11:02,988 --> 00:11:04,014
in this decade,

351
00:11:04,014 --> 00:11:05,724
with more bonds and cash,

352
00:11:05,724 --> 00:11:08,528
so a market crash right before retirement

353
00:11:08,528 --> 00:11:10,306
doesn't derail everything.

354
00:11:10,655 --> 00:11:13,280
For families headed by someone sixty five

355
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:14,304
to seventy four,

356
00:11:14,304 --> 00:11:16,544
the median net worth was about four

357
00:11:16,544 --> 00:11:18,401
hundred ten thousand dollars,

358
00:11:18,401 --> 00:11:20,257
the highest of any age group.

359
00:11:20,437 --> 00:11:21,971
For seventy five and older,

360
00:11:21,971 --> 00:11:24,245
about three hundred thirty six thousand.

361
00:11:24,594 --> 00:11:26,805
Net worth usually peaks in the late

362
00:11:26,805 --> 00:11:28,574
sixties and early seventies,

363
00:11:28,574 --> 00:11:31,227
then slowly declines as people spend their

364
00:11:31,227 --> 00:11:32,617
savings in retirement.

365
00:11:32,797 --> 00:11:33,888
That's not failure.

366
00:11:34,068 --> 00:11:35,903
That's exactly what the money was for.

367
00:11:36,083 --> 00:11:37,899
The goal in this stage isn't to

368
00:11:37,899 --> 00:11:38,660
keep growing.

369
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:40,414
It's to make the money last,

370
00:11:40,414 --> 00:11:41,313
and to enjoy it.

371
00:11:41,662 --> 00:11:44,530
What matters in this decade is managing

372
00:11:44,530 --> 00:11:47,177
withdrawals, taxes and health costs.

373
00:11:47,357 --> 00:11:51,260
Required minimum distributions from many retirement accounts

374
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:52,821
start in your seventies,

375
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:55,163
and the rules have changed in recent

376
00:11:55,163 --> 00:11:57,504
years, so check the current IRS age.

377
00:11:57,684 --> 00:11:59,921
Long term care is another big unknown,

378
00:11:59,921 --> 00:12:01,981
and planning for it early gives you

379
00:12:01,981 --> 00:12:02,746
more choices.

380
00:12:03,096 --> 00:12:04,802
If you're behind in retirement,

381
00:12:04,802 --> 00:12:06,452
focus on what you can control.

382
00:12:06,632 --> 00:12:08,167
Reduce big fixed costs,

383
00:12:08,167 --> 00:12:09,836
like housing, if you can.

384
00:12:10,016 --> 00:12:12,448
Look into every benefit you may qualify

385
00:12:12,448 --> 00:12:12,698
for.

386
00:12:12,878 --> 00:12:14,933
Consider part time work you enjoy.

387
00:12:15,113 --> 00:12:17,405
And be extremely careful with scams,

388
00:12:17,405 --> 00:12:19,316
which target retirees heavily.

389
00:12:19,496 --> 00:12:22,747
The FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center reports

390
00:12:22,747 --> 00:12:25,534
billions of dollars in losses among people

391
00:12:25,534 --> 00:12:26,264
over sixty.

392
00:12:26,614 --> 00:12:28,032
Your move in this stage:

393
00:12:28,032 --> 00:12:30,158
write a simple yearly spending plan,

394
00:12:30,158 --> 00:12:32,107
keep one to two years of spending

395
00:12:32,107 --> 00:12:33,111
in safe accounts,

396
00:12:33,111 --> 00:12:35,415
and review beneficiaries and your will.

397
00:12:35,595 --> 00:12:37,225
And if you have more than you

398
00:12:37,225 --> 00:12:39,136
need, decide while you can how you

399
00:12:39,136 --> 00:12:40,990
want to help family or causes you

400
00:12:40,990 --> 00:12:41,609
care about.

401
00:12:41,958 --> 00:12:43,324
Before the psychology,

402
00:12:43,324 --> 00:12:45,684
let's look at what actually builds net

403
00:12:45,684 --> 00:12:47,485
worth in American households.

404
00:12:47,665 --> 00:12:49,782
Because when you look at the Federal

405
00:12:49,782 --> 00:12:52,016
Reserve data, the same few things show

406
00:12:52,016 --> 00:12:53,133
up again and again.

407
00:12:53,483 --> 00:12:54,691
The first is the home.

408
00:12:54,871 --> 00:12:56,223
For the middle class,

409
00:12:56,223 --> 00:12:58,734
home equity is often the largest single

410
00:12:58,734 --> 00:12:59,120
asset.

411
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:01,723
Every mortgage payment pays down a bit

412
00:13:01,723 --> 00:13:02,489
of the loan,

413
00:13:02,489 --> 00:13:04,912
and over long periods home prices have

414
00:13:04,912 --> 00:13:05,869
tended to rise,

415
00:13:05,869 --> 00:13:08,228
though not always and not everywhere.

416
00:13:08,408 --> 00:13:10,411
That's why net worth often jumps for

417
00:13:10,411 --> 00:13:12,414
people in their forties and fifties.

418
00:13:12,594 --> 00:13:14,916
It's not that they suddenly became great

419
00:13:14,916 --> 00:13:15,496
investors.

420
00:13:15,676 --> 00:13:18,207
Their house quietly turned into savings.

421
00:13:18,557 --> 00:13:20,251
But the home is a slow,

422
00:13:20,251 --> 00:13:21,355
illiquid lever.

423
00:13:21,535 --> 00:13:23,503
You can't sell a bedroom to pay

424
00:13:23,503 --> 00:13:24,391
for groceries.

425
00:13:24,571 --> 00:13:26,946
And a house that's too expensive can

426
00:13:26,946 --> 00:13:29,123
drain your savings through taxes,

427
00:13:29,123 --> 00:13:30,574
insurance and repairs.

428
00:13:30,754 --> 00:13:32,725
The best version of this lever is

429
00:13:32,725 --> 00:13:34,755
a home you can comfortably afford,

430
00:13:34,755 --> 00:13:36,009
kept for a long time,

431
00:13:36,009 --> 00:13:38,219
with a mortgage you'll pay off before

432
00:13:38,219 --> 00:13:39,473
or around retirement.

433
00:13:39,822 --> 00:13:42,377
The second lever is retirement accounts.

434
00:13:42,557 --> 00:13:44,875
Workplace plans like a 401k,

435
00:13:44,875 --> 00:13:48,187
plus IRAs, let money grow without yearly

436
00:13:48,187 --> 00:13:52,576
taxes, and many employers add matching contributions.

437
00:13:52,756 --> 00:13:53,680
For many families,

438
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:55,785
these accounts and the home together make

439
00:13:55,785 --> 00:13:57,017
up most of their wealth.

440
00:13:57,197 --> 00:13:59,423
The secret here is automation:

441
00:13:59,423 --> 00:14:02,392
contributions taken from every paycheck,

442
00:14:02,392 --> 00:14:05,138
invested, and left alone for decades.

443
00:14:05,488 --> 00:14:07,822
The third lever is business ownership.

444
00:14:08,002 --> 00:14:09,980
In the Federal Reserve's data,

445
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:12,353
families that own a business tend to

446
00:14:12,353 --> 00:14:14,792
have much higher net worth than those

447
00:14:14,792 --> 00:14:17,626
that don't, though business is also riskier

448
00:14:17,626 --> 00:14:19,670
and many small businesses fail.

449
00:14:19,850 --> 00:14:21,452
You don't have to start a company

450
00:14:21,452 --> 00:14:21,986
to benefit.

451
00:14:22,166 --> 00:14:24,779
Owning broad stock index funds makes you

452
00:14:24,779 --> 00:14:27,391
a part owner of thousands of businesses.

453
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:30,110
And the fourth lever works in reverse:

454
00:14:30,110 --> 00:14:30,422
debt.

455
00:14:30,602 --> 00:14:32,952
High interest debt pulls net worth down

456
00:14:32,952 --> 00:14:34,097
every single month.

457
00:14:34,277 --> 00:14:36,382
A thirty thousand dollar car loan,

458
00:14:36,382 --> 00:14:39,169
a twenty thousand dollar credit card balance,

459
00:14:39,169 --> 00:14:41,212
and a large student loan can keep

460
00:14:41,212 --> 00:14:43,689
someone's net worth near zero for years,

461
00:14:43,689 --> 00:14:45,051
even on a good income.

462
00:14:45,231 --> 00:14:47,433
Paying down expensive debt is one of

463
00:14:47,433 --> 00:14:49,634
the most reliable ways to raise your

464
00:14:49,634 --> 00:14:52,142
net worth, because every dollar repaid is

465
00:14:52,142 --> 00:14:53,915
a dollar of net worth gained.

466
00:14:54,265 --> 00:14:55,867
So if you want to move your

467
00:14:55,867 --> 00:14:57,528
number, focus on these four.

468
00:14:57,708 --> 00:14:59,902
An affordable home kept long term.

469
00:15:00,082 --> 00:15:03,519
Automatic retirement investing with every matching dollar.

470
00:15:03,699 --> 00:15:06,566
Ownership, through funds or a business.

471
00:15:06,746 --> 00:15:08,848
And getting rid of expensive debt.

472
00:15:09,028 --> 00:15:11,222
Almost everything else is detail.

473
00:15:11,572 --> 00:15:13,661
Let's meet five example people,

474
00:15:13,661 --> 00:15:15,009
one for each decade.

475
00:15:15,189 --> 00:15:16,145
They're made up,

476
00:15:16,145 --> 00:15:17,938
but each one is built from the

477
00:15:17,938 --> 00:15:19,670
typical patterns in the data.

478
00:15:19,850 --> 00:15:21,905
See if you recognize yourself.

479
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:24,229
Jasmine is twenty eight.

480
00:15:24,409 --> 00:15:27,628
She has eighteen thousand dollars in savings

481
00:15:27,628 --> 00:15:28,432
and a 401k,

482
00:15:28,432 --> 00:15:30,408
a car worth eight thousand,

483
00:15:30,408 --> 00:15:33,407
and twenty two thousand in student loans.

484
00:15:33,587 --> 00:15:35,558
Her net worth is about plus four

485
00:15:35,558 --> 00:15:36,605
thousand dollars.

486
00:15:36,785 --> 00:15:39,195
Below the median for her age group,

487
00:15:39,195 --> 00:15:40,296
but moving fast,

488
00:15:40,296 --> 00:15:43,669
because she just started contributing ten percent

489
00:15:43,669 --> 00:15:45,528
of her salary with a match.

490
00:15:45,708 --> 00:15:46,568
In five years,

491
00:15:46,568 --> 00:15:47,735
if she keeps going,

492
00:15:47,735 --> 00:15:50,131
she'll likely be well above the median.

493
00:15:50,481 --> 00:15:52,153
David is thirty nine.

494
00:15:52,333 --> 00:15:53,957
He and his wife own a home

495
00:15:53,957 --> 00:15:56,579
with ninety thousand dollars of equity and

496
00:15:56,579 --> 00:15:59,264
have sixty thousand in retirement accounts.

497
00:15:59,444 --> 00:16:01,675
They also have a twenty eight thousand

498
00:16:01,675 --> 00:16:03,847
dollar car loan and eight thousand on

499
00:16:03,847 --> 00:16:04,669
a credit card.

500
00:16:04,849 --> 00:16:07,022
Their net worth is around one hundred

501
00:16:07,022 --> 00:16:08,549
fourteen thousand dollars,

502
00:16:08,549 --> 00:16:10,605
a little below the median for their

503
00:16:10,605 --> 00:16:10,840
age.

504
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:13,162
Paying off the card and not replacing

505
00:16:13,162 --> 00:16:14,842
the car for a few years would

506
00:16:14,842 --> 00:16:15,942
move them above it.

507
00:16:16,292 --> 00:16:18,626
Lisa is forty nine and single.

508
00:16:18,806 --> 00:16:20,654
She rents, has no home equity,

509
00:16:20,654 --> 00:16:23,117
and has about one hundred forty thousand

510
00:16:23,117 --> 00:16:25,273
dollars in her retirement accounts.

511
00:16:25,453 --> 00:16:27,419
Her net worth is about one hundred

512
00:16:27,419 --> 00:16:28,634
thirty five thousand.

513
00:16:28,814 --> 00:16:30,759
Below the median for her age,

514
00:16:30,759 --> 00:16:33,307
mostly because she doesn't own a home.

515
00:16:33,487 --> 00:16:35,701
But her money is liquid and invested,

516
00:16:35,701 --> 00:16:38,394
and with catch up contributions starting next

517
00:16:38,394 --> 00:16:40,070
year, she can put away a lot

518
00:16:40,070 --> 00:16:41,626
in her peak earning years.

519
00:16:41,975 --> 00:16:43,357
Mike is fifty eight.

520
00:16:43,537 --> 00:16:45,501
His house is almost paid off,

521
00:16:45,501 --> 00:16:48,550
with three hundred twenty thousand dollars of

522
00:16:48,550 --> 00:16:50,853
equity, and he has two hundred ten

523
00:16:50,853 --> 00:16:52,140
thousand in a 401k.

524
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:55,327
Net worth, about five hundred thirty thousand.

525
00:16:55,507 --> 00:16:57,086
Comfortably above the median.

526
00:16:57,266 --> 00:16:58,847
But his investable money,

527
00:16:58,847 --> 00:17:01,251
the part that will pay for retirement,

528
00:17:01,251 --> 00:17:03,338
is only two hundred ten thousand.

529
00:17:03,518 --> 00:17:06,072
Mike's real question isn't his net worth.

530
00:17:06,252 --> 00:17:07,953
It's whether to downsize,

531
00:17:07,953 --> 00:17:09,450
work a few more years,

532
00:17:09,450 --> 00:17:11,152
or delay Social Security.

533
00:17:11,501 --> 00:17:13,405
And Rosa is seventy one.

534
00:17:13,585 --> 00:17:15,760
Her net worth is about four hundred

535
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:18,369
thousand dollars, mostly her paid off home

536
00:17:18,369 --> 00:17:20,667
plus one hundred thousand in savings.

537
00:17:20,847 --> 00:17:23,404
With Social Security covering most of her

538
00:17:23,404 --> 00:17:25,837
bills, she withdraws a little each year

539
00:17:25,837 --> 00:17:27,895
for travel and her grandchildren.

540
00:17:28,075 --> 00:17:30,360
Her net worth is slowly declining,

541
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:32,243
and that's exactly the plan.

542
00:17:32,423 --> 00:17:34,083
Her money is doing its job.

543
00:17:34,433 --> 00:17:36,486
Notice that the net worth number alone

544
00:17:36,486 --> 00:17:38,485
doesn't tell you who's in good shape.

545
00:17:38,665 --> 00:17:41,492
Jasmine's small number is healthy because of

546
00:17:41,492 --> 00:17:42,392
her direction.

547
00:17:42,572 --> 00:17:45,266
Mike's big number hides a retirement question.

548
00:17:45,446 --> 00:17:47,872
Rosa's falling number is a success.

549
00:17:48,052 --> 00:17:49,863
That's why the playbook matters more than

550
00:17:49,863 --> 00:17:50,525
the comparison.

551
00:17:50,875 --> 00:17:51,746
Angle two.

552
00:17:51,926 --> 00:17:52,936
The psychology.

553
00:17:53,116 --> 00:17:55,423
Because looking at these numbers does something

554
00:17:55,423 --> 00:17:55,914
to people.

555
00:17:56,094 --> 00:17:57,348
Some feel proud.

556
00:17:57,528 --> 00:17:58,852
Some feel crushed.

557
00:17:59,032 --> 00:18:00,964
And how you react matters more than

558
00:18:00,964 --> 00:18:01,957
the number itself.

559
00:18:02,307 --> 00:18:04,770
The first trap is comparing yourself to

560
00:18:04,770 --> 00:18:06,917
the average instead of the median.

561
00:18:07,097 --> 00:18:09,255
People see a headline that the average

562
00:18:09,255 --> 00:18:11,414
American in their forties has nearly a

563
00:18:11,414 --> 00:18:13,970
million dollars, and they feel like failures.

564
00:18:14,150 --> 00:18:15,981
But that average is pulled up by

565
00:18:15,981 --> 00:18:18,271
a small number of very wealthy families.

566
00:18:18,451 --> 00:18:20,212
Compare yourself to the median,

567
00:18:20,212 --> 00:18:22,712
and most people discover they're much closer

568
00:18:22,712 --> 00:18:24,303
to normal than they thought.

569
00:18:24,652 --> 00:18:26,944
The second trap is comparing yourself to

570
00:18:26,944 --> 00:18:28,205
the people around you.

571
00:18:28,385 --> 00:18:29,970
Your neighbor's new truck,

572
00:18:29,970 --> 00:18:31,372
your friend's vacation,

573
00:18:31,372 --> 00:18:33,017
your coworker's renovation.

574
00:18:33,197 --> 00:18:34,285
You see their spending,

575
00:18:34,285 --> 00:18:35,183
but not their debt.

576
00:18:35,363 --> 00:18:37,227
Some of the people who look the

577
00:18:37,227 --> 00:18:39,273
richest have the lowest net worth,

578
00:18:39,273 --> 00:18:41,679
because they spend everything they earn.

579
00:18:41,859 --> 00:18:44,424
Stanley and Danko found exactly this in

580
00:18:44,424 --> 00:18:45,411
their research.

581
00:18:45,591 --> 00:18:48,544
Many true millionaires lived in modest homes

582
00:18:48,544 --> 00:18:50,154
and drove ordinary cars.

583
00:18:50,503 --> 00:18:52,048
The third trap is shame.

584
00:18:52,228 --> 00:18:54,065
When people feel far behind,

585
00:18:54,065 --> 00:18:55,641
they often stop looking.

586
00:18:55,821 --> 00:18:57,570
They avoid opening statements,

587
00:18:57,570 --> 00:18:59,086
they don't check balances,

588
00:18:59,086 --> 00:19:01,069
and the problem grows in the dark.

589
00:19:01,249 --> 00:19:03,610
Research on financial stress has found that

590
00:19:03,610 --> 00:19:05,586
worry about money can make it harder

591
00:19:05,586 --> 00:19:07,948
to think clearly about long term decisions.

592
00:19:08,128 --> 00:19:09,820
The fix is to look anyway,

593
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:11,773
and turn a scary number into a

594
00:19:11,773 --> 00:19:12,098
plan.

595
00:19:12,448 --> 00:19:14,762
The fourth trap is the opposite:

596
00:19:14,762 --> 00:19:15,630
complacency.

597
00:19:15,810 --> 00:19:18,745
Some people discover they're above the median

598
00:19:18,745 --> 00:19:19,397
and relax.

599
00:19:19,577 --> 00:19:21,690
But the median isn't a finish line.

600
00:19:21,870 --> 00:19:23,994
A median sixty year old with three

601
00:19:23,994 --> 00:19:25,930
hundred sixty thousand dollars,

602
00:19:25,930 --> 00:19:27,304
mostly in home equity,

603
00:19:27,304 --> 00:19:29,927
may not have enough to retire comfortably.

604
00:19:30,107 --> 00:19:31,958
Being normal isn't the same as being

605
00:19:31,958 --> 00:19:32,267
ready.

606
00:19:32,447 --> 00:19:34,188
Your target is your own number,

607
00:19:34,188 --> 00:19:35,198
not the country's.

608
00:19:35,548 --> 00:19:37,673
So here's the healthy way to use

609
00:19:37,673 --> 00:19:38,602
these numbers.

610
00:19:38,782 --> 00:19:39,943
Look at them once.

611
00:19:40,123 --> 00:19:41,005
Find your decade.

612
00:19:41,185 --> 00:19:43,665
Notice whether you're above or below the

613
00:19:43,665 --> 00:19:44,099
median.

614
00:19:44,279 --> 00:19:45,448
Then put them away,

615
00:19:45,448 --> 00:19:48,339
because staring at other people's numbers every

616
00:19:48,339 --> 00:19:49,446
week helps nobody.

617
00:19:49,626 --> 00:19:52,155
Focus on the only comparison that really

618
00:19:52,155 --> 00:19:54,557
matters: you this year versus you last

619
00:19:54,557 --> 00:19:54,873
year.

620
00:19:55,053 --> 00:19:56,709
If your net worth is going up

621
00:19:56,709 --> 00:19:58,251
every year, you're winning,

622
00:19:58,251 --> 00:19:59,964
whatever anyone else is doing.

623
00:20:00,314 --> 00:20:03,254
There's one more psychological effect worth knowing

624
00:20:03,254 --> 00:20:03,600
about.

625
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:06,100
When people start tracking their net worth

626
00:20:06,100 --> 00:20:08,585
regularly, they tend to make better decisions

627
00:20:08,585 --> 00:20:09,689
without even trying.

628
00:20:09,869 --> 00:20:12,261
A purchase stops being just a purchase.

629
00:20:12,441 --> 00:20:14,171
It becomes a small dent in a

630
00:20:14,171 --> 00:20:15,529
number you care about.

631
00:20:15,709 --> 00:20:18,478
Economists sometimes call this salience:

632
00:20:18,478 --> 00:20:20,277
what you pay attention to,

633
00:20:20,277 --> 00:20:21,038
you manage.

634
00:20:21,218 --> 00:20:23,577
That's why the single most useful habit

635
00:20:23,577 --> 00:20:25,936
from this whole video is simply writing

636
00:20:25,936 --> 00:20:26,964
your number down,

637
00:20:26,964 --> 00:20:27,569
regularly.

638
00:20:27,919 --> 00:20:30,360
And finally, be kind to yourself about

639
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:30,938
the past.

640
00:20:31,118 --> 00:20:33,275
Many people who are behind got there

641
00:20:33,275 --> 00:20:35,492
through things they couldn't control:

642
00:20:35,492 --> 00:20:36,751
a layoff, an illness,

643
00:20:36,751 --> 00:20:38,489
a divorce, caring for family,

644
00:20:38,489 --> 00:20:40,766
or starting adult life in a recession.

645
00:20:40,946 --> 00:20:43,146
Your number reflects your history,

646
00:20:43,146 --> 00:20:44,893
not your worth as a person.

647
00:20:45,073 --> 00:20:47,388
What matters now is the next decision,

648
00:20:47,388 --> 00:20:48,788
and the one after that.

649
00:20:49,138 --> 00:20:50,032
Angle three.

650
00:20:50,212 --> 00:20:50,955
The playbook.

651
00:20:51,135 --> 00:20:53,027
If you're behind for your age,

652
00:20:53,027 --> 00:20:55,109
here's a catch up plan that works

653
00:20:55,109 --> 00:20:55,803
at any age.

654
00:20:55,983 --> 00:20:57,445
Five moves, in order.

655
00:20:57,795 --> 00:20:58,550
Move one.

656
00:20:58,730 --> 00:20:59,949
Find your real number.

657
00:21:00,129 --> 00:21:02,268
Write down everything you own and everything

658
00:21:02,268 --> 00:21:02,997
you owe, today.

659
00:21:03,177 --> 00:21:04,866
Then calculate two versions:

660
00:21:04,866 --> 00:21:06,134
your total net worth,

661
00:21:06,134 --> 00:21:08,366
and your net worth without your home.

662
00:21:08,546 --> 00:21:10,479
The second one tells you how much

663
00:21:10,479 --> 00:21:12,471
money is actually working for you.

664
00:21:12,651 --> 00:21:13,881
Do this once a quarter.

665
00:21:14,061 --> 00:21:15,720
It takes less than an hour,

666
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:17,993
and watching the number change is one

667
00:21:17,993 --> 00:21:19,959
of the best motivators there is.

668
00:21:20,309 --> 00:21:21,273
Move two.

669
00:21:21,453 --> 00:21:22,811
Stop the leaks.

670
00:21:22,991 --> 00:21:25,464
High interest debt is the biggest one.

671
00:21:25,644 --> 00:21:28,510
Credit cards at over twenty percent interest

672
00:21:28,510 --> 00:21:30,985
are compounding against you every day.

673
00:21:31,165 --> 00:21:32,798
Then the big fixed costs:

674
00:21:32,798 --> 00:21:34,822
a car payment that's too large,

675
00:21:34,822 --> 00:21:36,651
a home that eats too much of

676
00:21:36,651 --> 00:21:39,524
your income, subscriptions you forgot about.

677
00:21:39,704 --> 00:21:41,682
Every leak you plug becomes money you

678
00:21:41,682 --> 00:21:42,270
can invest.

679
00:21:42,619 --> 00:21:43,606
Move three.

680
00:21:43,786 --> 00:21:45,374
Raise your savings rate,

681
00:21:45,374 --> 00:21:47,292
aggressively but sustainably.

682
00:21:47,472 --> 00:21:48,521
If you're behind,

683
00:21:48,521 --> 00:21:50,619
ten percent probably isn't enough.

684
00:21:50,799 --> 00:21:52,033
Aim for fifteen,

685
00:21:52,033 --> 00:21:52,958
then twenty.

686
00:21:53,138 --> 00:21:55,747
Use raises, bonuses and paid off loans

687
00:21:55,747 --> 00:21:58,700
to get there without cutting your lifestyle

688
00:21:58,700 --> 00:21:59,524
all at once.

689
00:21:59,704 --> 00:22:02,429
And use every tax advantaged account you

690
00:22:02,429 --> 00:22:03,451
have access to:

691
00:22:03,451 --> 00:22:04,950
workplace plans, IRAs,

692
00:22:04,950 --> 00:22:08,016
and catch up contributions once you're fifty.

693
00:22:08,366 --> 00:22:09,202
Move four.

694
00:22:09,382 --> 00:22:10,567
Grow your income.

695
00:22:10,747 --> 00:22:11,551
At any age,

696
00:22:11,551 --> 00:22:13,966
more income is the fastest way to

697
00:22:13,966 --> 00:22:14,624
catch up.

698
00:22:14,804 --> 00:22:16,844
In your twenties and thirties,

699
00:22:16,844 --> 00:22:19,564
it's skills, job changes and promotions.

700
00:22:19,744 --> 00:22:21,427
In your forties and fifties,

701
00:22:21,427 --> 00:22:23,290
it might be a raise you've been

702
00:22:23,290 --> 00:22:24,371
afraid to ask for,

703
00:22:24,371 --> 00:22:25,753
consulting on the side,

704
00:22:25,753 --> 00:22:27,616
or turning a hobby into income.

705
00:22:27,796 --> 00:22:30,153
Even a few hundred extra dollars a

706
00:22:30,153 --> 00:22:32,510
month, invested, changes the math.

707
00:22:32,859 --> 00:22:33,823
Move five.

708
00:22:34,003 --> 00:22:36,000
Let time and consistency work.

709
00:22:36,180 --> 00:22:38,911
Invest automatically, in low cost,

710
00:22:38,911 --> 00:22:42,605
diversified funds, and don't stop when markets

711
00:22:42,605 --> 00:22:43,007
fall.

712
00:22:43,187 --> 00:22:45,214
At a seven percent illustration,

713
00:22:45,214 --> 00:22:48,318
someone who starts investing one thousand dollars

714
00:22:48,318 --> 00:22:50,345
a month at forty five could have

715
00:22:50,345 --> 00:22:53,322
roughly five hundred twenty thousand dollars by

716
00:22:53,322 --> 00:22:54,019
sixty five.

717
00:22:54,199 --> 00:22:56,117
That alone would put them well above

718
00:22:56,117 --> 00:22:57,450
the median for their age.

719
00:22:57,799 --> 00:23:00,536
On screen: one thousand dollars a month,

720
00:23:00,536 --> 00:23:02,110
starting at forty five,

721
00:23:02,110 --> 00:23:03,273
for twenty years,

722
00:23:03,273 --> 00:23:05,462
at a seven percent illustration.

723
00:23:05,642 --> 00:23:08,455
Roughly five hundred twenty thousand dollars at

724
00:23:08,455 --> 00:23:09,113
sixty five.

725
00:23:09,463 --> 00:23:12,050
Every decade has one mistake that costs

726
00:23:12,050 --> 00:23:13,840
people more than any other.

727
00:23:14,020 --> 00:23:14,852
Here they are,

728
00:23:14,852 --> 00:23:16,099
so you can skip them.

729
00:23:16,448 --> 00:23:17,511
In your twenties,

730
00:23:17,511 --> 00:23:20,013
the biggest mistake is waiting to start.

731
00:23:20,193 --> 00:23:22,496
People think they'll invest once they earn

732
00:23:22,496 --> 00:23:22,770
more.

733
00:23:22,950 --> 00:23:25,162
But the money invested in your twenties

734
00:23:25,162 --> 00:23:27,316
is the most powerful money you'll ever

735
00:23:27,316 --> 00:23:29,358
invest, because it has the most time

736
00:23:29,358 --> 00:23:29,812
to grow.

737
00:23:29,992 --> 00:23:32,221
At a seven percent illustration,

738
00:23:32,221 --> 00:23:34,728
a dollar invested at twenty five can

739
00:23:34,728 --> 00:23:37,584
grow roughly sixteen times by sixty five.

740
00:23:37,764 --> 00:23:40,573
A dollar invested at forty five grows

741
00:23:40,573 --> 00:23:41,863
about four times.

742
00:23:42,043 --> 00:23:42,995
Same dollar.

743
00:23:43,175 --> 00:23:44,498
Very different result.

744
00:23:44,848 --> 00:23:45,954
In your thirties,

745
00:23:45,954 --> 00:23:48,749
the biggest mistake is lifestyle inflation.

746
00:23:48,929 --> 00:23:50,427
Income finally rises,

747
00:23:50,427 --> 00:23:52,424
and so does everything else.

748
00:23:52,604 --> 00:23:53,974
The house, the cars,

749
00:23:53,974 --> 00:23:56,099
the vacations, the restaurants.

750
00:23:56,279 --> 00:23:57,683
It all feels deserved.

751
00:23:57,863 --> 00:23:59,954
But the thirties are when the gap

752
00:23:59,954 --> 00:24:02,298
between savers and spenders opens up.

753
00:24:02,478 --> 00:24:05,151
Keep your lifestyle growing slower than your

754
00:24:05,151 --> 00:24:07,885
income, and your forties will look completely

755
00:24:07,885 --> 00:24:08,492
different.

756
00:24:08,842 --> 00:24:09,887
In your forties,

757
00:24:09,887 --> 00:24:12,758
the biggest mistake is putting everyone else

758
00:24:12,758 --> 00:24:13,150
first.

759
00:24:13,330 --> 00:24:16,085
Kids' activities, college savings,

760
00:24:16,085 --> 00:24:17,382
helping parents.

761
00:24:17,562 --> 00:24:19,663
All of it comes from love.

762
00:24:19,843 --> 00:24:21,718
But a common piece of advice from

763
00:24:21,718 --> 00:24:23,707
planners is that you can borrow for

764
00:24:23,707 --> 00:24:26,263
college, but you can't borrow for retirement.

765
00:24:26,443 --> 00:24:28,122
Keep funding your own future,

766
00:24:28,122 --> 00:24:30,090
even while you help the people you

767
00:24:30,090 --> 00:24:30,379
love.

768
00:24:30,729 --> 00:24:31,761
In your fifties,

769
00:24:31,761 --> 00:24:33,632
the biggest mistake is panic.

770
00:24:33,812 --> 00:24:35,994
Either panic that it's too late,

771
00:24:35,994 --> 00:24:38,039
which leads people to give up,

772
00:24:38,039 --> 00:24:40,289
or panic that leads them to chase

773
00:24:40,289 --> 00:24:42,879
risky investments to catch up quickly.

774
00:24:43,059 --> 00:24:44,302
Both are expensive.

775
00:24:44,482 --> 00:24:46,326
The fifties reward steady,

776
00:24:46,326 --> 00:24:47,816
boring, high savings,

777
00:24:47,816 --> 00:24:50,937
using every catch up contribution available.

778
00:24:51,286 --> 00:24:52,335
In your sixties,

779
00:24:52,335 --> 00:24:55,416
the biggest mistake is claiming Social Security

780
00:24:55,416 --> 00:24:56,400
without a plan,

781
00:24:56,400 --> 00:24:59,612
or retiring without knowing your health insurance

782
00:24:59,612 --> 00:25:00,005
costs.

783
00:25:00,185 --> 00:25:02,667
These decisions are mostly permanent,

784
00:25:02,667 --> 00:25:05,282
so they deserve real time and research.

785
00:25:05,462 --> 00:25:07,614
And in your seventies and beyond,

786
00:25:07,614 --> 00:25:10,287
the biggest mistake is falling for scams,

787
00:25:10,287 --> 00:25:12,960
or never letting yourself enjoy the money

788
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:14,460
you worked so hard for.

789
00:25:14,810 --> 00:25:16,560
Here's a challenge I want to give

790
00:25:16,560 --> 00:25:16,772
you.

791
00:25:16,952 --> 00:25:18,694
Forget the medians for a moment.

792
00:25:18,874 --> 00:25:20,671
Ask yourself one question:

793
00:25:20,671 --> 00:25:22,745
where could my net worth be in

794
00:25:22,745 --> 00:25:23,437
ten years?

795
00:25:23,786 --> 00:25:25,331
Start with today's number.

796
00:25:25,511 --> 00:25:27,958
Then add what you'll invest each year,

797
00:25:27,958 --> 00:25:30,213
and a conservative growth estimate.

798
00:25:30,393 --> 00:25:32,365
At a seven percent illustration,

799
00:25:32,365 --> 00:25:34,583
five hundred dollars a month for ten

800
00:25:34,583 --> 00:25:37,295
years grows to roughly eighty seven thousand

801
00:25:37,295 --> 00:25:37,788
dollars.

802
00:25:37,968 --> 00:25:39,736
One thousand dollars a month,

803
00:25:39,736 --> 00:25:42,357
roughly one hundred seventy three thousand.

804
00:25:42,537 --> 00:25:43,951
Add debt you'll pay off,

805
00:25:43,951 --> 00:25:45,955
and equity you'll build in a home,

806
00:25:45,955 --> 00:25:48,194
and most people are surprised how much

807
00:25:48,194 --> 00:25:49,549
can change in a decade.

808
00:25:49,899 --> 00:25:52,278
Ten years is long enough for compounding

809
00:25:52,278 --> 00:25:53,468
to start showing up,

810
00:25:53,468 --> 00:25:55,252
and short enough to feel real.

811
00:25:55,432 --> 00:25:57,777
It's also roughly the gap between the

812
00:25:57,777 --> 00:26:00,250
age groups in the Federal Reserve data.

813
00:26:00,430 --> 00:26:02,119
So your challenge is this:

814
00:26:02,119 --> 00:26:02,963
in ten years,

815
00:26:02,963 --> 00:26:04,977
be above the median for the age

816
00:26:04,977 --> 00:26:06,212
group you'll be in.

817
00:26:06,392 --> 00:26:08,079
If you're thirty two today,

818
00:26:08,079 --> 00:26:09,953
aim to beat the forty two year

819
00:26:09,953 --> 00:26:10,641
old median.

820
00:26:10,821 --> 00:26:11,924
If you're fifty,

821
00:26:11,924 --> 00:26:14,130
aim to beat the median at sixty.

822
00:26:14,479 --> 00:26:16,201
Write your ten year target on a

823
00:26:16,201 --> 00:26:17,034
piece of paper,

824
00:26:17,034 --> 00:26:19,144
along with the monthly amount it takes

825
00:26:19,144 --> 00:26:19,866
to get there.

826
00:26:20,046 --> 00:26:21,556
Then check it once a year,

827
00:26:21,556 --> 00:26:22,543
on the same date.

828
00:26:22,723 --> 00:26:24,692
Some years you'll be ahead,

829
00:26:24,692 --> 00:26:25,567
some behind.

830
00:26:25,747 --> 00:26:26,804
Markets will move.

831
00:26:26,984 --> 00:26:27,878
Life will happen.

832
00:26:28,058 --> 00:26:30,137
But having a clear target turns net

833
00:26:30,137 --> 00:26:32,156
worth from a scary judgment into a

834
00:26:32,156 --> 00:26:33,166
game you can win.

835
00:26:33,515 --> 00:26:35,879
And here's what people often find after

836
00:26:35,879 --> 00:26:37,091
a few years of this.

837
00:26:37,271 --> 00:26:39,457
The number starts to grow faster than

838
00:26:39,457 --> 00:26:41,996
they expected, because growth begins to add

839
00:26:41,996 --> 00:26:43,355
to their contributions.

840
00:26:43,535 --> 00:26:44,986
The habits get easier.

841
00:26:45,166 --> 00:26:47,049
And the question changes from,

842
00:26:47,049 --> 00:26:47,802
am I behind?

843
00:26:47,982 --> 00:26:49,990
to, how far ahead can I get?

844
00:26:50,170 --> 00:26:52,411
That shift, from fear to curiosity,

845
00:26:52,411 --> 00:26:54,332
is one of the best things that

846
00:26:54,332 --> 00:26:56,509
can happen to your financial life.

847
00:26:56,859 --> 00:26:59,193
Let me answer the questions people ask

848
00:26:59,193 --> 00:27:01,097
me most about net worth by age.

849
00:27:01,447 --> 00:27:02,307
Question one.

850
00:27:02,487 --> 00:27:03,717
Should I count my house?

851
00:27:03,897 --> 00:27:05,829
For your total net worth,

852
00:27:05,829 --> 00:27:06,138
yes.

853
00:27:06,318 --> 00:27:08,482
But also track your net worth without

854
00:27:08,482 --> 00:27:10,939
the house, because you can't buy groceries

855
00:27:10,939 --> 00:27:12,986
with home equity unless you sell or

856
00:27:12,986 --> 00:27:14,039
borrow against it.

857
00:27:14,219 --> 00:27:15,229
Question two.

858
00:27:15,409 --> 00:27:16,662
Should I count my car?

859
